; Regression (2026-09-20): taking the ADDRESS of a function that takes an
; implicit caller page. The address-of path mangles with the page the source
; DECLARES; the definition's name comes from function_needs_caller_page#, which
; also reads the BODY. Where the two disagree the reference names a symbol
; nothing defines — measured on this very file with the previous compiler:
;
;   define linkonce_odr { ptr } @_Z14makes_a_stringPN4scaly6memory4PageE(ptr %0)
;   declare ptr @_Z14makes_a_stringv(...)
;
; and the only evidence was `ld: symbol(s) not found`, with no line and no
; relation to the change that caused it (widening one Option deep in the call
; graph is enough to move a definition's name).
;
; ★The address is handed to an EXTERN here, and that is not decoration: with
; `let p &f` followed by `if p = null` the planner folds the test to false, the
; reference never reaches the IR and the link succeeds — so a fixture that only
; BINDS the address proves nothing about the class.
;
; ★It is a DIAGNOSTIC and not a corrected mangling on purpose: the helpers this
; bites are pinned to a (void*, void*) C ABI by the code that calls them
; through the pointer, so a page-taking definition is an ABI break whatever it
; is called. `fn_ref_atexit.scaly` is the control — `&fn` on an
; allocation-free function stays legal.
; xfail: error: cannot take the address of makes_a_string

procedure atexit(cb: pointer[void]) returns int extern

function makes_a_string() returns String
    String("this allocates, so the function takes a caller page")

atexit(&makes_a_string)
